
find /home/user -name "myfile.txt"    --> Find a file by name (case-sensitive)


find / -iname "myfile.txt"   -->  Find a file by name (case-insensitive)


find . -type f -name "*.log"  --> Only find regular files (not directories)


find . -user viswa 2>/dev/null   --> to search based on user
find / -not -user viswa -type f 2>/dev/null
find / -uid 1001 -type f 2>/dev/null      --> to serach using user id

find /home -user viswa -type f -ls 2>/dev/null  --> to show details of files owned by viswa


find . -group flm 2>/dev/null     --> to search based on group
find / -not -group flm -type f 2>/dev/null
find / -gid 1002 -type f 2>/dev/null      --> to serach using group id

find /home -group flm -type f -ls 2>/dev/null  --> to show details of files owned by group


find . -perm 777    --> to search based on permissions


find / -type f -size +100M   --> Find files by size (e.g., larger than 100 MB)


find /path/to/search -type f -mtime -7    --> Find files modified within last 7 days


find . -type f -exec grep -l "search_string" {} \;   --> Combine find with grep to search file contents , -l -> Only print the filename, not the matching line.
                          -Hn                        --> -H    → print filename , -n    → print line number
                                                     --> {} represents each file found by find
                                                     --> \; Marks the end of the -exec command

explanation:
============
find .
   ↓
Find everything under current directory
   ↓
-type f
   ↓
Keep only regular files
   ↓
-exec
   ↓
Run grep on each file
   ↓
grep -l "viswa"   --> -l Only print the filename, not the matching line.
     -Hn          --> -H    → print filename , -n    → print line number
   ↓
Print only files containing "viswa"
